          %h2#Idea Anyon braiding
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %em braid group statistics
            or
            %em anyon statistics
            (sometimes:
            = succeed ")" do
              %em plektons
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+statistics"} particle statistics
            where the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            of certain effective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particles"} particles
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyons"}> anyons
            ”) in an effectively 1+2-dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            has the effect of transforming the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            of the total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            which constitute a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
            – a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representation"} braid representation
          %p
            Often this is motivated as a generalization of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} boson
            = succeed "-\u201Cstatistics\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#AsGeneralizedBosonFermionStatistics"} below
          %p
            But the actual mathematical nature of anyons must be different from that of elements of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} boson
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fock+spaces"} Fock spaces
            and often remains somewhat vague in existing discussions. One may recognize two different more concrete conceptualizations of
            %em anyons
            in the literature:
          %ol
            %li#AnyonicQuanta
              %p
                %strong anyonic quanta
                much like
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boson"} boson
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermion"} fermion
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quanta"} quanta
                but subject to an additional global
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
                by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aharonov-Bohm+effect"} Aharonov-Bohm phases
                due to a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fictitious+gauge+field"} fictitious gauge field
                which is sourced by and coupled to each of the quanta;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (this goes back to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#ArovasSchriefferWilczekZee85"} Arovas, Schrieffer, Wilczek &amp; Zee 1985
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"} Chen, Wilczek, Witten &amp; Halperin 1989
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#AsAFictitiousAharonovBohmEffect"} below
            %li#AnyonicDefects
              %p
                %strong anyonic defects
                like
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
                or other
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parameterized+quantum+system"} classical parameter
                to the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+system"} quantum system
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic movement
                of which acts by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phases"} Berry phases
                on the quantum
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (e.g.
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#ArovasSchriefferWilczek84"} Arovas, Schrieffer &amp; Wilczek 1984
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a{:href => "#AsBraidingOfDefects"} further below
          %p
            The concept of anyons is particularly well motivated in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            are common place (eg.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graphene"} graphene
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+Hall+effect"} quantum Hall effect
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+%28in+theoretical+physics%29"} models
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+theory"} condensed matter theory
            (mostly
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+models"} lattice models
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string-net+models"} string-net models
            %em theoretically
            realize anyon braid group statistics, and there are some first
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            indications of anyonic phenomena in actual materials (see the references under
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#ObservationOfAnyonsInFQH"} Experimental Realization
          %p
            Specifically, in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+phases+of+matter"} topological phases of matter
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+order"} topological order
          %p
            Besides general curiosity, much of the interest in anyonic braid group statistics lies in the fact that these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+representations"} braid representations
            are imagined to potentially serve as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gates"} quantum gates
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computers"} topological quantum computers
          %h3#AsGeneralizedBosonFermionStatistics As generalized boson/fermion statistics?
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
            species when referring to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            that
            = succeed "-particle" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            form under the the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(n)
            which
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation"} permutes
            the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            particles.
          %p
            While there is a rich
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory+of+the+symmetric+group"} representation theory of the symmetric group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            says, when it applies, that for
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
              quanta
            only the simplest two possibilities may occur:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bosons"} bosons
                (such as
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photons"} photons
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+representation"} trivial representation
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sym
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(n)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
                (such as
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electrons"} electrons
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sign+representation"} sign representation
                of
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Sym
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sym(n)
              %p
                (see also at
                = succeed ")" do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Slater+determinant"} Slater determinant
          %p
            Now, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            strands covers the symmetric group
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Br
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↠
                  %mi Sym
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Br(n)
                  \\twoheadrightarrow
                  Sym(n)
          %p
            which allows one to regard any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} linear representation
            of the symmetric group also as a particular
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representation"} braid group representation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
            may be understood as the group of
            = succeed "-classes" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/disjoint+union"} disjoint
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/timelike"} timelike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            in an effectively 2+1 dimensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concatenation"} concatenation
            of worldlines.
          %p
            In this sense, one may imagine that
            %em any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representations"} braid group representations
            may generalize the boson/fermion statistics in 2+1 dimensions. Texts typically suggest that this applies to
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiparticle"} quasiparticles
          %p
            The term
            %em anyon
            (due to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Wilczek82b"} Wilczek 1982b
            %em any
            statistics “in between” boson- and fermion-statistics may be allowed.
          %p
            On the other hand, anyonic braiding is conceptually different from boson/fermion statistics – if it were on the same footing then the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin-statistics+theorem"} spin-statistics theorem
            would
            %em rule out
            anyonic braiding. This is acknowledged by
            %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"} Chen, Wilczek, Witten &amp; Halperin 1989, p. 352
            (cf. also
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Wilczek90"} Wilczek 1990, §I.2, pp. 11
          %blockquote
            %p
              Once the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+group"} permutation group
              is replaced by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#AsAFictitiousAharonovBohmEffect Braiding of anyonic quanta – via “fictitious” AB-phases
          %p
            A concrete model for
            %em
              %a{:href => "#AnyonicQuanta"} anyonic quanta
            via otherwise
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            in 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            through a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fictitious+gauge+field"}> fictitious gauge field
            ” was proposed in
            %a{:href => "#ArovasSchriefferWilczekZee85"} Arovas, Schrieffer, Wilczek &amp; Zee 1985
            and developed in
            %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"} Chen, Wilczek, Witten &amp; Halperin 1989
            and
            %a{:href => "#IengoLechner92"} Iengo &amp; Lechner 1992
            (the model has been advertized in early reviews, e.g.
            %a{:href => "#Wilczek90"} Wilczek 1990, §I.3
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wilczek91"} Wilczek 1991
          %p
            This model regards anyons as
            %em a priori
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field"} free
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fermions"} fermions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            via a “fictitious gauge field” (
            %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"}> CWWH89, §2
            ), in that each of the particles is modeled as the singular source of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+connection"} flat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+bundle+with+connection"} circle connection
            (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+potential"} vector potential
            with vanishing
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+force"} Lorentz force
            but has the effect that globally each other particle is subject to the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aharonov-Bohm+effect"} Aharonov-Bohm effect
            as would be caused by a tuple of infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solenoids"} solenoids
            piercing through each of the other particle’s positions.
          %p
            For emphasis, from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"} CWWH89, p. 359
          %blockquote
            %p
              Here the particles are to be regarded (in the absence of interactions) as fermions; the interaction then makes them anyons with statistical parameter
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi θ
                      %mo =
                      %mi π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta = \pi(1 - 1/n)
          %p
            It follows (
            %a{:href => "#Wu84"}> Wu 1984
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ImboImboSudarshan90"} Imbo, Imbo &amp; Sudarshan 1990
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#FroehlichGabbianiMarchetti90"} Fröhlich, Gabbiani &amp; Marchetti 1990, p. 20
          %blockquote
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mspace{:width => "negativethinmathspace"}
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \in\!\!\!\!\!/ \frac{1}{2}\mathbb{Z}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+quantum+states"} Hilbert space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anyon"} anyon
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+functions"} wave functions
              must be chosen to be a space of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-valued+functions"} multi-valued functions
              with half-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monodromies"} monodromies
              given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+phase"} phase
              factors
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 2
                      %mi π
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} i
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} exp(2 \pi \mathrm{i} \theta)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi M
                        %mo ˜
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde M_n
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi M
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M_n
              \&amp;lbrack;the
              = succeed "&rbrack." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
          %p
            Further discussion of anyon-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multi-valued+functions"} multi-valued functions
            on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+functions"} equivariant functions
            on its
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+cover"} universal cover
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BCMS93"} BCMS93, §1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MundSchrader93"} Mund &amp; Schrader 1995
            %a{:href => "#DFT97"} DFT97, §1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Myrheim99"} Myrheim 1999
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#DMV03"} DMV03
            %a{:href => "#MurthyShankar09"} Murthy &amp; Shankar 2009
          %p
            Incidentally, the
            = succeed "-excitations" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-particle"} quasi-particle
            %em of
            (or
            = succeed ")" do
              %em in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
            (
            %a{:href => "#ChenWilczekWittenHalperin89"}> CWWH89, p. 457
            ):
          %blockquote
            %p
              we are led to conclude that
              %em in anyon superconductivity, charged quasi-particles and vortices do not constitute two separate sorts of elementary excitations - they are one and the same.
          %p This seamlessly leads over to:
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#AsBraidingOfDefects Braiding of anyonic defects – via adiabatic Berry phases
          %p
            In practice, many (most?) incarnations of the concept of anyons are
            %em
              %a{:href => "#AnyonicDefects"} anyonic defects
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} solitonic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            (of
            = succeed "=2)," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
            in fluids:
          %blockquote
            %p
              %em Anyonic particles are best viewed as a kind of topological defects that reveal non-trivial properties of the ground state.
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#Kitaev06"}> Kitaev 2006, p. 4
              \&amp;rbrack;
            %p
              %em Anyons can arise in two ways: as localised excitations of an interacting quantum Hamiltonian or as defects in an ordered system.
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "topological+quantum+computation#DasSarmaFreedmanNayak15"}> Das Sarma, Freedman &amp; Nayak 2015, p. 1
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            (Compare also the original discussions in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GoldinMenikoffSharp81"} Goldin, Menikoff &amp; Sharp 1981, §III
            %a{:href => "#Wilczek82a"} Wilczek 1982a
            \&amp;
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Wilczek90"} Wilczek 1990, p. 5
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+state"} bound
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solenoid"} solenoid
            – hence a 2d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+monopole"} magnetic monopole
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect"} defect
            – as a decent model for an anyon.)
          %p
            But
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            are a kind of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+conditions"} boundary conditions
            %em external parameters
            or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+fields"} background fields
            for the actual
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field"} quantum field
          %p
            Concretely, a widely appreciated proposal (
            %a{:href => "#MooreRead91"}> Moore &amp; Read 1991
            ,
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#ReadRezayi99"} Read &amp; Rezayi 1999
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunctions"} wavefunctions
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+blocks"} conformal blocks
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFT"} 2d CFT
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence"} hence
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+states"} states
            – with prescribed poles at the location of the anyons.
          %p
            Now the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+adiabatic+theorem"} quantum adiabatic theorem
            says that the sufficiently slow motion of such external parameters transforms the quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operators"} unitary operators
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berry+phases"}> Berry phases
            ”, see also at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+quantum+computation"} adiabatic quantum computation
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ArovasSchriefferWilczek84"} Arovas, Schrieffer &amp; Wilczek 1984, p. 1
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FreedmanKitaevLarsenWang03"} Freedman, Kitaev, Larsen &amp; Wang 2003, pp. 6
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#NayakSimonSternFreedman08"} Nayak, Simon, Stern &amp; Freedman 2008, §II.A.2 (p. 6)
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "adiabatic+quantum+computation#ChengGalitskiDasSarma11"} Cheng, Galitski &amp; Das Sarma 2011, p. 1
          %p
          #DefectBraiding.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (adiabatic defect braiding)
              %br/
              %em
                Anyon braiding statistics is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group+representation"} braid group representation
                on a quantum
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+state"} ground state
                induced by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braiding"} braiding
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/TQFT"} topological
                = succeed "=2" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
                in their
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space
            %p Here
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  for point-defects the “configuration space” is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/configuration+space+of+points"} configuration space of points
                  in a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surface"} surface
                  (as briefly touched upon already in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#LeinaasMyrheim77"} Leinaas &amp; Myrheim 1977, pp. 22
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a{:href => "#Wilczek82b"} Wilczek 1982b, p. 959
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane"} plane
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid group
              %li
                %p
                  “topological” is meant as in
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theory"} topological quantum field theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+transformation"} unitary transformation
                  is demanded/assumed to depend only on the
                  = succeed "-class" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isotopy"} isotopy
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
                  = succeed "-pattern." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+group"} braid
            %p
          %p
            This notion of anyon “statistics” is at least tacitly implicit in much of the literature on anyons in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldlines"} worldlines
            as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BraidingOfNodalPointsInMomentumSpace"} below
          %p
            Indeed, the effects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adiabatic+theorem"} adiabatic
            braiding of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defects"} defects
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"} quantum materials
            has been understood and discussed before and in parallel to the term “anyon” becoming established:
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Mermin79"} Mermin 1979
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#LoPreskill93"} Lo &amp; Preskill 1993
          %p
            A concrete realistic example of defect anyons are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortex"} vortex
            anyons see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#VortexAnyons"} below
            = succeed "-excitations" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasiparticle"} quasiparticle
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#AnyonicBandNodes"} anyonic band nodes
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "#DefectBranes"}> defect branes
                \.
          %h4#VortexAnyons Vortex anyons
          %p
            Specifically,
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortex"} vortex
              anyons
            are realized in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bose-Einstein+condensates"} Bose-Einstein condensates
            (
            %a{:href => "#MPSS19"}> MPSS19
            , following
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#PFCZ01"} PFCZ01
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superfluids"} superfluids
            (
            %a{:href => "#MMN21"}> MMN21
            ).
          %p#VorticesInAnyonCondensate
            In fact, defect-type
            %em vortex anyons
            generically appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensates"} condensates
            %em of non-defect anyons
            (
            %a{:href => "#CDLR19"}> CDLR19
            ):
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/VorticesInAnyonCondensate.jpg", :width => "430px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                From
                %a{:href => "#CDLR19"} CDLR19
          %p
            A theoretical model of vortex anyons in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higgs+field"} Higgs field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+theory"} Chern-Simons theory
            is discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#FroehlichMarchetti88"} Fröhlich &amp; Marchetti 1988
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+model"} lattice model
            of vortex anyons is analyzed in detail in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Kitaev06"} Kitaev 2006
          %p
            Much attention in current efforts towards realizing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"} topological quantum computation
            is being paid to anyons realized as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Majorana+zero+modes"} Majorana zero modes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+state"} bound
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
            (
            %a{:href => "topological+quantum+computation#DasSarmaFreedmanNayak15"}> Das Sarma, Freedman &amp; Nayak 2015
            , cf.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "su2-anyon#MMBDRSC19"} MMBDRSC19
          %p#MayGeneralize
            This situation may generalize to
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parafermion"} parafermion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/su%282%29-anyons"} su(2)-anyons
          %blockquote
            %p
              each (anti)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} soliton
              carries
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parafermion"} parafermion
              zero mode which supplies it with the non-Abelian statistics &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "parafermion#Tsvelik2014a"}> Tsvelik 2014a
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/1404.2840.pdf#page=2"} p. 2
              = succeed "&rbrack;." do
                %a{:href => "parafermion#Borcherding18"} Borcherding 2018, pp. 3
          %p#BraidingOfNodalPointsInMomentumSpace
            %strong Braiding of nodal points in momentum space
            (graphics from
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "su2-anyon#MMBDRSC19"} MMBDRSC19
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-019-10397-5.pdf#page=2"} Fig. 1
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/MZMVortex-220530.jpg", :width => "440px"}/
          %h4#AnyonicBandNodes Anyonic band nodes?
          %p
            Around 2020 the view has been emerging that also defects “in momentum/reciprocal space” may behave as anyonic defects under braiding in momentum space. This applies concretely to nodal points (where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron+bands"} electron bands
            touch or cross) in the momentum space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brillouin+torus"} Brillouin torus
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+semi-metals"} topological semi-metals
          %blockquote
            %p
              here are band crossing points, henceforth called
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vortices"} vortices
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#AhnParkYang19"}> Ahnm Park &amp; Yang 2019
              \&amp;rbrack;
            %p
              a new type non-Abelian “braiding” of nodal-line rings inside the momentum space &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#TiwariBzdusek20"}> Tiwari &amp; Bzdušek 2020
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %div{:style => "margin: -20px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/BraidingOfBandNodes-220529.jpg", :width => "500px"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics from
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Topological+Quantum+Computation+in+TED-K"} SS22
          %p Curiously, these reciprocal/momemtum space anyons lend themselves to tractable laboratory manipulation in a way that has remained notoriously elusive for “position space” anyons:
          %blockquote
            %p
              Our work opens up routes to readily manipulate Weyl nodes using only slight external parameter changes, paving the way for the practical realization of reciprocal space braiding &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#CBSM22"}> CBSM22
              \&amp;rbrack;,
          %p specifically
          %blockquote
            %p
              it is possible to controllably braid Kagome band nodes in monolayer
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} Si
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} O
                      %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{Si}_2 \mathrm{O}_3
              using strain and/or an external electric field &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#PBMS22"}> PBMS22
              \&amp;rbrack;,
          %p leading to:
          %blockquote
            %p new opportunities for exploring non-Abelian braiding of band crossing points (nodes) in reciprocal space, providing an alternative to the real space braiding exploited by other strategies.
            %p
              Real space braiding is practically constrained to boundary states, which has made experimental observation and manipulation difficult; instead, reciprocal space braiding occurs in the bulk states of the band structures and we demonstrate in this work that this provides a straightforward platform for non-Abelian braiding. &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "#PBSM22"}> PBSM22
              \&amp;rbrack;.
          %h4#DefectBranes Defect branes
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/defect+branes"} defect branes
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
            of
            = succeed "=2," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codimension"} codimension
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D7-branes"} D7-branes
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIB+string+theory"} type IIB string theory
            or
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane+intersection"} intersections
            on “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3-brane+in+6d"}> M3-branes
            ” in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
          %p
            It has been suggest in
            %a{:href => "defect+brane#deBoerShigemori12"} deBoer &amp; Shigemori 2012, p. 65
            that these could behave like anyons. This is further substantiated in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "defect+brane#SS22"} SS22
            %a{:href => "defect+brane#RelationToAnyons"} there
            for more.
          %p
            %br/
